+{"id":"ex-r-and-open-interval-homeomorphic-not-uniformly-isomorphic","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The homeomorphism h(x)=x/(1+|x|) is a standard bounded-coordinate example; searches found only informal exact discussions, not an allowed source for this witness. The verification shows established mathematics, not novelty. Its triangle-inequality citation is wrong. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: The bounded-coordinate homeomorphism x/(1+|x|), its explicit inverse, and the inverse's failure of uniform continuity near the endpoints form a canonical metric/uniform-space example. The current witness is standard and directly recoverable, not AI-invented.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"fs-a-compatible-uniformity-is-unique","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://math.berkeley.edu/~wodzicki/H104.F13/UniformStructure-alt.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Nonuniqueness of compatible uniformities on noncompact spaces is standard; Wodzicki proves uniqueness only compactly and gives continuous-not-uniformly-continuous witnesses. No source states this exact false universal plus pullback refutation. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: A topology need not determine a unique compatible uniformity, and pulling back the interval's metric uniformity along the canonical homeomorphism gives the standard counterexample mechanism. The local false-statement package is established mathematics.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"ex-additive-group-uniformity-on-r","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":["https://u.math.biu.ac.il/~megereli/TGrNotes070217.pdf","https://homepages.math.uic.edu/~rosendal/PapersWebsite/Coarse-Geometry-Book23.pdf"],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The sourced group formulas and commutativity make all four group uniformities equal the usual metric uniformity on additive R. No source states this exact four-way example, but it is standard, not novel. ALPHA CONCURRENCE: In an abelian topological group the left and right uniformities coincide, so their upper join and Roelcke meet coincide as well; on additive R the common entourages are exactly the usual metric ones. This is a canonical worked instance of standard definitions.","alpha_concurred":true,"at":"2026-08-08"}
